# FireRouter

An open-source, low-latency streaming proxy that sits in front of Fireworks AI's
OpenAI-compatible API (https://api.fireworks.ai/inference/v1/chat/completions).
Apps send requests to FireRouter instead of Fireworks directly.

## Eventual features (NOT all to be built now; we build in small steps)
- Streaming SSE passthrough with no buffering (tokens forwarded the instant they arrive)
- Per-tenant rate limiting (requests/min and tokens/min, token-bucket algorithm)
- Circuit breaker per upstream model with automatic fallback to a backup model
- Tuned HTTP/2 connection pooling to the upstream
- Prometheus metrics, including time-to-first-token (TTFT) and proxy overhead
- Benchmark tooling: a mock upstream and a streaming-aware load generator

## Stack and rules
- Go 1.22+. Standard library first. Ask before adding any dependency.
- Layout: cmd/<binary>/main.go for executables, internal/<pkg> for libraries.
- NEVER buffer an SSE response. Forward each chunk and Flush() immediately.
  Never use io.ReadAll or bufio.Scanner on the response stream path.
- Always propagate request context to upstream calls (client disconnect must
  cancel the upstream request).
- Never log prompts or API keys. Never hardcode keys; read from environment.
- Share one http.Transport per process. Never create a client per request.
- Inject time (a `now` parameter or clock interface) in anything time-dependent
  so it is testable.
- Every package gets tests. Run `go vet ./... && go test -race ./...` before
  saying a task is done.
- Keep functions small. Comment WHY, not what. The author is new to Go, so
  prefer clear code over clever code.
- Request bodies are forwarded as raw JSON. Parse only the fields we need
  (model, stream, max_tokens, messages for estimation). Never drop or
  reorder unknown fields.
- When rewriting the model field for fallback, change only that field.
- Every new endpoint goes through the same auth, rate limit, breaker and
  transport as chat completions.

## Working style
- Do exactly what the current prompt asks. Do not build ahead.
- If a requirement is ambiguous, ask before guessing.
- After finishing, summarize what you changed and how to run it.
- Do not create a git branch. Commit and push on `main`. Create a branch only when the user explicitly says to. Do not open a pull request unless the user explicitly asks for one.
